08:14.943 --> 08:23.947
[SPEAKER_02]: unpopular, but I discovered this through personal experience, and I've since talked to multiple very good friends who are also SIV

08:23.987 --> 08:34.951
[SPEAKER_02]: instructors. That is just the very basics of how to deploy a reserve in an emergency. And what people are taught throughout their beginner

08:35.011 --> 08:43.674
[SPEAKER_02]: courses and then also through their, I think, generally older SIV instructors, reach for the handle, grab it, pull it across your body and

08:44.714 --> 08:54.683
[SPEAKER_02]: throw it outside. Or just grab it with one hand and do some kind of motion that involves throwing it to the side. Yeah, that's wrong, outdated

08:54.723 --> 09:09.052
[SPEAKER_00]: information. Or tell me what's the... I would suggest just pull it in one swish, like one go, and then just throw it as far as possible. The problem

09:09.072 --> 09:20.588
[SPEAKER_02]: with that is most reserve handles have some variable length of bridle attachment to the bag. When you do that, you can pull it in one motion, but

09:20.768 --> 09:30.135
[SPEAKER_02]: then what's actually throwing it out is just... a little bit right at the end because a whole bunch of your arm's range of motion is taken up just

09:30.895 --> 09:42.420
[SPEAKER_02]: pulling the bridle that's connected to the pod. But the point of this is doing it and they throw it out to the side. And the number of people who've

09:42.520 --> 09:53.066
[SPEAKER_02]: died or had very hard landings from their reserve getting tangled with the wing or taking a long time to deploy using that process is quite high.

09:53.746 --> 10:06.894
[SPEAKER_02]: There was a guy a couple of years ago in either Germany or Austria with A or B wing. doing big wing overs and he mistimes it and then goes gets all gets

10:06.954 --> 10:17.941
[SPEAKER_02]: a frontal and then twisted and he throws his reserve it takes quite a while to like finally come out and then gets fucked up into the lines and

10:18.001 --> 10:29.346
[SPEAKER_02]: he lands in the trees very hard that video was posted and he died in the hospital a couple days later and is a very good example of like core

10:29.406 --> 10:41.271
[SPEAKER_02]: technique in in my opinion in 2018 i was testing how to deploy a cluster of reserves it was four reserves packed in bags then packed in a bigger bag

10:41.651 --> 10:51.919
[SPEAKER_02]: and attached to a pilot chute also soaked in about five liters each of gasoline and diesel and obviously they're all soaking wet and so I did a

10:51.939 --> 10:59.308
[SPEAKER_02]: whole bunch of tests just with water packing reserves dunking a whole bunch of water and then taking it to the landing area and seeing like the

10:59.428 --> 11:11.216
[SPEAKER_02]: best most effective way to deploy them and to get as close to 100% success rate as possible with stoking wet reserves. One of the things I found is

11:11.376 --> 11:24.825
[SPEAKER_02]: the initial snatch makes a huge difference. Whether it's a slow release from the bag and then catching wind, or in that case, I went out in very

11:24.865 --> 11:37.549
[SPEAKER_02]: strong winds, ran to a static attachment point to the bag and then ripped it out and shared it into the wind. And doing that, I got 100% success rate

11:37.729 --> 11:47.132
[SPEAKER_02]: soaking wet. And that was a very good example of that initial snatch and release and catching the wind being the most important part instead of

11:47.192 --> 11:56.337
[SPEAKER_02]: it just slowly coming out, either dropping it or not having a good throw. That led me to my current belief in most emergency scenarios,

11:56.737 --> 12:04.922
[SPEAKER_02]: especially if you feel any G forces, the best thing is to grab the reserves, bring it to the front, grab it with two hands, open your legs

12:05.282 --> 12:15.608
[SPEAKER_02]: and throw it right down between your legs as hard as you can. Grabbing the bag, not the handle. Do you think there's enough time to go through all of

12:15.628 --> 12:26.316
[SPEAKER_00]: that in any reserve deployment scenarios? Yeah, that just adds an extra maybe two seconds. But when you weigh that against the many videos of

12:26.996 --> 12:35.899
[SPEAKER_02]: quick release doing the single motion thing, a lot of times the bag basically ends up just dropping straight down. And when that happens,

12:36.120 --> 12:44.387
[SPEAKER_02]: it just gives the wing so much more time to suck up the reserve before the reserve has actually... It drops down and then it still has to rotate

12:44.687 --> 12:54.734
[SPEAKER_02]: slowly all the way up as the wing is spinning around, sucking up lines before it actually... inflates and gets some snatch force from the wind

12:55.114 --> 13:06.137
[SPEAKER_02]: as opposed to instantly being released and inflating. The downside to that is then you will get flipped upside down for a couple seconds. In

13:06.197 --> 13:17.399
[SPEAKER_02]: most cases, like in a lot of emergencies where you have enough vertical descent rate to get injured or die, like the G forces are coming straight

13:17.880 --> 13:30.773
[SPEAKER_02]: down. There's the wing and there's you. And the best way to ensure a clean deployment is not to throw it sideways. It's to throw it down. Because

13:31.573 --> 13:41.635
[SPEAKER_02]: the other thing that a lot of schools teach is throw to the outside of the turn. But if you're twisted or if you have a negative rotation, it is very

13:41.695 --> 13:50.572
[SPEAKER_02]: difficult in an emergency to know which direction you should throw it in. And a lot of people end up screwing that up. So you're telling me that

13:50.692 --> 13:59.938
[SPEAKER_00]: most scenarios you will be having feet down, face up situation. Then you just throw them under your legs and that is the best deployment

14:00.098 --> 14:09.652
[SPEAKER_00]: solution. For true emergency ones, if you can feel G- forces, the only one this doesn't apply to is being in a stall, like a stable rotation with

14:11.333 --> 14:24.542
[SPEAKER_02]: line twists. How does this work in twisted nose-down autorotation? How do you apply this? Yeah, when you're nose-down, there's going to be

14:24.702 --> 14:32.931
[SPEAKER_02]: quite a bit of G-force going down. Yeah. So if you throw it out, you're never going to be able to know which is the correct direction to throw it

14:32.991 --> 14:43.805
[SPEAKER_02]: out. Just throw it sideways with one hand, like the chances of the wing catching the reserve and the reserve lines before it actually gets some

14:43.865 --> 14:53.076
[SPEAKER_02]: snatch force and some inflation is quite high. But the G- forces are too damn high to just bring it inside the cockpit and do all of that. A lot is

14:53.116 --> 15:01.020
[SPEAKER_00]: going through your body. I remember when I deployed in that scenario, I was barely able to get to the reserve handle. Forget about bringing it to

15:01.060 --> 15:09.344
[SPEAKER_00]: the front and doing everything else with it. So all I did was get to it and release it. The spiral was so strong that eventually, no matter where I

15:09.364 --> 15:18.590
[SPEAKER_00]: threw it, it still went up because the spiral was so quick. It was minus 23 or something momentarily. You don't necessarily need to bring it to the

15:18.650 --> 15:27.859
[SPEAKER_02]: center and throw it down with both hands. That has been my emergency plan. You can also, with one hand, throw it straight down instead of

15:27.899 --> 15:37.387
[SPEAKER_02]: trying to throw it to the side. There's a guy named Matt Wilkes who I met at Garda one time and he's a PhD and he's done a whole bunch of reserve

