[SPEAKER_02]: wing. They haven't seen if it's stable or if they're just going to be in a pendulum swinging back and forth the whole time. I want to ask, does

24:34.565 --> 24:44.028
[SPEAKER_00]: bigger reserve mean slower descent rate? If as a solo guy, if you throw a tandem reserve, does it mean that you come down softer? It's for sure.

24:44.269 --> 24:54.532
[SPEAKER_02]: You want shorter lines. You want lighter material. You want gear that you will take with you. That's easy to maintain. That's easy to dry and

24:54.592 --> 25:07.416
[SPEAKER_02]: pack and actually use. And tandem reserve for normal people, I don't think makes sense. And I don't know anyone using it, getting one rated

25:07.476 --> 25:17.677
[SPEAKER_02]: for five or 10 kilos more than what you would ever fly with all of your gear included. is a good thing. Because one of the very scary moments I had

25:17.717 --> 25:27.880
[SPEAKER_02]: throwing a reserve was one rated for 80 kilos and was an ultralight round. The descent rate was probably eight meters a second. Scary fast.

25:29.542 --> 25:39.009
[SPEAKER_00]: Between choosing the shapes and sizes, there's also this thickness or whatever you can call it, the lightness or the normal fabric. And I don't

25:39.049 --> 25:46.995
[SPEAKER_00]: recall what fabric we use in reserves, but a lot of times it's zero porosity as well. There are different manufacturers offering

25:47.035 --> 25:56.841
[SPEAKER_00]: different fabric choices. So when it comes to you as trying to choose a reserve, what kind of fabric offers the best safety Otherwise, what

25:56.881 --> 26:04.985
[SPEAKER_00]: kind of fabric do you recommend people look out for? It's a good question. Basically, you have the normal weight fabrics that are more

26:05.005 --> 26:15.030
[SPEAKER_02]: or less F111. There's the ultralight. There's the trade-off that lighter fabric results in lighter total package, which in theory will

26:15.070 --> 26:26.996
[SPEAKER_02]: inflate faster and will get out, catch wind faster. They saw that a lot in base parachutes when they went from full weight fabric to ultralight

26:27.076 --> 26:34.481
[SPEAKER_02]: ones, there was a few meter difference for static line openings and that snatch force when you throw your pilot sheet and it gets pulled out of the

26:34.521 --> 26:44.167
[SPEAKER_02]: container and gets to line stretch. In the short term, I think ultralight is better and safer and results in a faster deployment. The

26:44.188 --> 26:55.155
[SPEAKER_02]: trade-off is long-term after abuse or being left in a hot car or possibly being stored a little bit wet. Landing in a tree is another thing.

26:56.096 --> 27:07.709
[SPEAKER_02]: Recently, there's at least one manufacturer that is making a reserve with line attachment points with the lines just looped on so you can

27:07.849 --> 27:18.438
[SPEAKER_02]: easily swap them out instead of them being permanently stitched. If you have a tree landing, you have to send it in instead of just requesting

27:18.458 --> 27:26.364
[SPEAKER_02]: however many replacement lines or a whole replacement mindset, which I think is a good idea. And then also gives you the option because there's

27:26.524 --> 27:35.391
[SPEAKER_02]: Spectra and then there's another line, thicker, heavier line variants and to be able to swap between them depending on what your priorities

27:35.451 --> 27:45.056
[SPEAKER_02]: are. There's a very interesting point on reserve attachment points that I want to talk about. But before I get there, this thought came to my

27:45.096 --> 27:53.500
[SPEAKER_00]: mind about the reusability of reserves. So let's say when they get wet, when you have a freshwater deployment, let's say there's no saltwater

27:53.540 --> 28:01.303
[SPEAKER_00]: exposure, how many times do you think a reserve can have a safe cycle of deployments if you keep using it on SIVs or something and keep landing in

28:01.323 --> 28:09.151
[SPEAKER_00]: freshwater, but are taking the precautions and packing it well and everything? Yeah, I think it really depends because it's not just going

28:09.231 --> 28:19.753
[SPEAKER_02]: in the water. Technically, the fabric could probably go in the water 50 times. If you keep it indoors, dunk it into your bathtub, gently let it

28:19.793 --> 28:30.396
[SPEAKER_02]: drip dry a little bit and then spread it out and let it dry indoors in the shade versus what a lot of people do. They land in the water, may or may not

28:30.536 --> 28:40.574
[SPEAKER_02]: pull it back into the boat. very well. One of the worst things you can do to fabric is drag it or pull it when it has a whole bunch of water in it instead

28:40.594 --> 28:49.864
[SPEAKER_02]: of very gently pulling it so that the water drains out like pulling it from the apex or the tail and just like super slowly and gently instead of

28:49.884 --> 28:54.589
[SPEAKER_02]: if you're impatient and your friend is helping you or something and they're just pulling it out because they don't know any better. That's

28:54.729 --> 29:02.875
[SPEAKER_02]: one big part. And then the second is how they dry. I see a lot of people here at Garda and they're in a rush. And sometimes there'll be eight wings,

29:03.076 --> 29:12.222
[SPEAKER_02]: eight reserves, all connected to a chain with 25, 30 kilometers of winds, keeping them inflated. And they dry very fast, but there's a lot

29:12.242 --> 29:19.848
[SPEAKER_02]: of abrasion. There's a lot of reserve lines just getting completely twisted or tangled with other reserves right next to it. And that adds a

29:19.888 --> 29:28.625
[SPEAKER_02]: lot of taking it out and leaving it in the shade or indoors to dry over time. two days. Interesting way of having a factory of reserve

29:28.665 --> 29:43.718
[SPEAKER_00]: repacking. Yeah, it depends. But I would say for most people who like on the far end of the abuse side, still easily 15, 20 deployments, I would

29:43.738 --> 29:52.945
[SPEAKER_02]: say, are the ballpark. So most reserves are being thrown out as trash before they're reaching the end of their lifespan in most cases. Yeah,

29:53.186 --> 30:04.612
[SPEAKER_02]: either being thrown out or destroyed in trees or depending where you're actually using it. Yeah. Anyways, I think, and 10 years age span, is that

30:04.652 --> 30:11.854
[SPEAKER_00]: like a definite no-go that no matter what happens, you're not exceeding adult? Because to me, material does not really seem to deteriorate at

30:11.914 --> 30:19.438
[SPEAKER_00]: all. 10 years is just a recommended one and not really a forced one. So what's your take on that, please, if you can tell our audiences? That's

30:19.538 --> 30:30.490
[SPEAKER_02]: finding the lowest common denominator, the worst set of circumstances or the worst user, applying the rules to that person. Like someone who

30:30.510 --> 30:41.119
[SPEAKER_02]: keeps their gear in the car in the summer all year or might store it wet or in a damp place as opposed to someone who... keeps their gear dry,

30:41.580 --> 30:51.251
[SPEAKER_02]: doesn't use it in saltwater, limits exposure to sand, getting in there and abrasion, and then storing it somewhere at good room temperature in

30:51.291 --> 31:02.742
[SPEAKER_02]: a dry place. I've used an own gear that's closer to 20 years and still works fine after serious abuse but making sure that it's dry and like

31:02.783 --> 31:12.243
[SPEAKER_02]: sometimes I'll leave gear just spread out through my house for days to make sure it's 100% dry before packing it Damn, that's dedication, bro.

31:12.263 --> 31:19.630
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, and it's funny when you weren't the one who got it wet, and people get your gear wet, and they're just like, sorry about that. Okay, I'm

31:19.710 --> 31:22.593
[SPEAKER_02]: out. You can't walk around your house for a couple days.

31:25.856 --> 31:31.802
[SPEAKER_00]: I think this is such a fascinating talk, so we can always keep on going in this tangent. But I quickly want to come back to reserve attachment

31:31.822 --> 31:41.429
[SPEAKER_00]: points. I was talking to Eric from Fineo. And for audiences, that episode is also in post-production. He has filed an open patent where

31:41.509 --> 31:49.134
[SPEAKER_00]: the attachment points are coming out from carabiners and not from the shoulder position in the usual pod harnesses. That's basically an open

31:49.354 --> 31:56.799
[SPEAKER_00]: stand-up system or something where the pilot gets to stand up more easily with more visibility. Currently, there hasn't been any further

31:57.059 --> 32:06.127
[SPEAKER_00]: innovation from the position of shoulder straps. So this attachment points, do you think we are looking at a revolution of some sort? Or are

32:06.167 --> 32:12.313
[SPEAKER_00]: there any benefits of having different attachment points in your opinion? Because you have been attached from all shapes and

32:12.353 --> 32:23.743
[SPEAKER_00]: directions. Yeah, that leads me to a bigger topic. There's such a wide range of implementations of reserves in harnesses. There's no general

32:23.803 --> 32:32.131
[SPEAKER_02]: standard. And there's a lot of mixing and matching between components that sometimes have bad results. Just the attachment point on the

32:32.632 --> 32:41.139
[SPEAKER_02]: container, on the diaper, whether it's straight in line coming out, or if it's underneath, on top, or on the side, And some of those

32:41.179 --> 32:47.921
[SPEAKER_02]: combinations, and this is why people should try it, because when you actually try it with your handle, sometimes the whole bag, because of

32:47.941 --> 32:57.424
[SPEAKER_02]: the shape of it or the shape of the actual slot inside the harness, it just pivots and then torques against it and really resists the movement of

32:57.664 --> 33:10.475
[SPEAKER_02]: your arm of a normal throw. for harnesses. Sometimes you see people having thrown their reserve and the attachment points are coming from

33:10.515 --> 33:17.903
[SPEAKER_02]: behind their shoulders and they're pinned with their head forwards locked. And sometimes with the regalo, the handles are up here, but they

33:17.923 --> 33:27.168
[SPEAKER_02]: can't see anything. They go all the way to the ground like that. Then there's the debate of breaking your legs versus breaking your back. So

33:27.428 --> 33:37.917
[SPEAKER_02]: technically breaking your legs or crash landing legs first is the best way to absorb a lot of impact. And that's where the original concept of

33:38.018 --> 33:44.523
[SPEAKER_02]: having the attachment points on your shoulder comes from. But then for harnesses that have really good back protection, you definitely want

33:44.543 --> 33:55.390
[SPEAKER_02]: to use it. save your legs and not have your head pinned and it comes out and you're just you're not having this massive like body rotation that is a

33:55.410 --> 34:03.355
[SPEAKER_02]: little bit disorienting and then like potentially completely trapping you face down as you're trying to disable the wing that's

34:03.635 --> 34:12.201
[SPEAKER_02]: fitting somehow i feel that when you're using these big harnesses like i fly an xr7 myself a lot of times when i've seen that by flaring or coming

34:12.241 --> 34:22.006
[SPEAKER_02]: into land or in a pendulum the extra weight adds a lot more momentum than it would be in any other harness. So from that, I feel the, let's say the

34:22.026 --> 34:28.710
[SPEAKER_00]: harness itself weighs eight kilograms. And with your body weight, that's eight kilograms more adding to whatever G-force you're

34:28.910 --> 34:36.655
[SPEAKER_00]: enduring. I think you're going to come down hard in a big harness because of the weight. And of course there is protection, but in real life, in

34:36.695 --> 34:43.139
[SPEAKER_00]: real use case scenario, is this protection really even protecting? Because it's just making the impact even harder from what it is supposed

34:43.159 --> 34:52.063
[SPEAKER_00]: to be. Yeah, that's a good point. And there have been some unintentional downsides to some of the innovation in protection. If you remember,

34:52.083 --> 35:01.708
[SPEAKER_02]: there was the honeycomb sort of plastic protection that was supposed to be the next big thing. But I remember reading a few incident reports of

35:01.748 --> 35:10.876
[SPEAKER_02]: people who had very hard landings and just happened to impact at the correct angle where it did not deform, it did not crush, it did not absorb

35:10.976 --> 35:21.590
[SPEAKER_02]: any impact, and it just transferred it directly to the person's spine. There is always that trade-off, and the classic... combination of some

35:21.670 --> 35:35.981
[SPEAKER_02]: foam, some hard stiffened plastic to prevent punctures, and then some amount of compressible airbag that can slowly release, we'll say

35:36.021 --> 35:48.830
[SPEAKER_02]: slowly, but release air on impact to have multiple layers of protection is probably still the best all-round combination. Just having one of

35:48.870 --> 35:57.601
[SPEAKER_02]: those has downsides. If you just have foam, either your harness is going to be massive and super heavy, or you're just going to have a tiny bit of

35:57.621 --> 36:06.766
[SPEAKER_02]: foam. If you just have an airbag, then if you crash right after takeoff, you have nothing. Why don't we invent something like what is that the

36:06.826 --> 36:14.392
[SPEAKER_00]: packaging bubble sheet, like the one which we all love bursting at the time of moving. So that something like that, which has got like small

36:14.432 --> 36:23.718
[SPEAKER_00]: little airbags, which deform in a certain pattern upon impact, and they just find their whatever slow impact absorption that is something that

36:23.758 --> 36:30.341
[SPEAKER_00]: will first of all make it very clear about how the impact happened and then cover it from all angles and what's your take on that because i was

36:30.361 --> 36:38.025
[SPEAKER_00]: talking to fly neo owner eric yesterday we were recording an episode and he was the one who was responsible for bringing choreoid in the

36:38.065 --> 36:46.529
[SPEAKER_00]: paragliding market and he was basically talking about his journey Not at all, because I have been wanting to ask him, and I did ask him the same

36:46.569 --> 36:53.592
[SPEAKER_00]: question. How did it happen that the initial few use case scenarios of Courier turned out to have devastating consequences for some of the

36:53.612 --> 37:00.255
[SPEAKER_00]: people involved? He did admit that they made a mistake in the beginning and they corrected the product. He did admit that there were some

37:00.655 --> 37:08.198
[SPEAKER_00]: instances like this. So it was a case of technology gone wrong, which is scary in such a sport where equipment failures or technological

37:08.218 --> 37:17.704
[SPEAKER_00]: failures are not unheard of, but I believe something like that, some experimentation going off like that, and then having lives altered as a

37:17.744 --> 37:23.869
[SPEAKER_00]: community, you could have come up with some sort of control or I'm not exactly saying sanctions or something, but some kind of

37:23.949 --> 37:31.755
[SPEAKER_00]: accountability where a manufacturer is coming in with all bit of marketing and profit sharing in the hindsight with the nature of making

37:31.795 --> 37:43.722
[SPEAKER_00]: money and then just getting to walk out of it saying that, sorry, it was just a mistake. I have strong feelings about new innovations that break

37:43.802 --> 37:55.792
[SPEAKER_02]: the most basic law, which is do no harm. Whatever you introduce or create and put out into the world, specifically for safety, at the very least,

37:55.992 --> 38:08.756
[SPEAKER_02]: should not hurt people more than... We're going to be hurt already. And when a company puts something out that obviously was not tested

38:09.056 --> 38:22.579
[SPEAKER_02]: correctly and stacks up multiple injuries as a result, it's difficult to then believe them when they say, sorry, we fixed it. Trust us. And

38:22.639 --> 38:31.180
[SPEAKER_02]: yeah, I'm not sure. Because it is complicated, of course, but the testing for that, it's a lot easier than testing a lot of other things.

38:31.480 --> 38:41.546
[SPEAKER_02]: You can just create a whole bunch of templates of the general protection shape, put it on a harness, on a test dummy and a test rig and drop it a whole

38:41.566 --> 38:51.431
[SPEAKER_02]: bunch of times like they do for all the DHV testing. And for new products like that, you should do that hundreds of times. And I'd be very

38:51.491 --> 38:57.161
[SPEAKER_02]: surprised if... People don't crash that much. You should be able to cover.

39:00.100 --> 39:12.406
[SPEAKER_02]: the basic crash impact scenarios and vectors using a test rig. And for something that very clearly has this obvious sort of direct failure

39:12.426 --> 39:21.290
[SPEAKER_02]: mode where when there's any kind of an angle to the impact, it's going to crush a bit more. But when there's something that really doesn't want to

39:21.330 --> 39:30.992
[SPEAKER_02]: crush, that sort of should have been caught before, in my opinion. I want to give Eric a little bit of slack here because of the reply that he gave.

39:31.032 --> 39:36.935
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, it would have been a whole different conversation had we had this one-on-one with him. But his defense was that it's the only

39:36.975 --> 39:44.278
[SPEAKER_00]: protector where you can see where the damage is. All other protectors, you can't even. So you don't even know whether they've worked or not.

39:45.058 --> 39:55.381
[SPEAKER_00]: From what I believe it does, but it has such a blatant limitation compared to other ones which are supposed to work in whatever little

39:55.421 --> 40:05.025
[SPEAKER_00]: capacities. Innovation is always good. Sometimes it seems like the solution looking for a problem. To solve foam and airbags, you can look

40:05.045 --> 40:15.553
[SPEAKER_02]: at all the stitches and feel the foam. Being able to see the deformation after an impact, that's useful. The question then is how many people

40:15.673 --> 40:23.519
[SPEAKER_02]: actually replace it versus seeing a little bit of deformation. You're on a trip or you don't want to spend 30 bucks and you're like, that's okay,

40:23.539 --> 40:33.410
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll put it back in. There are pros and cons to having a potentially one-time use protector. I see a lot of people after a hard impact with a

40:33.450 --> 40:42.235
[SPEAKER_02]: helmet should replace the helmet. I see so many helmets with complete dents going in them. And I'm like, nice helmet, bro. And they're like,

40:42.255 --> 40:50.841
[SPEAKER_02]: that's fine. If you could see all the people with choroid protectors, I'm pretty sure you'd see a lot of that as well. This goes back to the do no

40:50.901 --> 41:00.027
[SPEAKER_02]: harm theory. In a perfect world with probably in Germany, probably all Germans replace them after you trip on takeoff, a little bit on your

41:00.067 --> 41:07.457
[SPEAKER_02]: butt, and they're like, nope, no more flying. I'm grounded for a few weeks until I get a new one. But most people don't fly like that. And I've

41:07.477 --> 41:14.548
[SPEAKER_02]: had a few really good discussions with some manufacturers who have this idea in their head. As an engineer, as a designer, you design

41:14.909 --> 41:22.478
[SPEAKER_02]: intentionally or unintentionally, but you're inherently biased. So if you're a very strict regimented kind of person, you're like, yes,

41:22.919 --> 41:33.010
[SPEAKER_02]: this is the best possible one case use or one use solution that will save them. And then they will definitely order a new one. In the real world, it

41:33.030 --> 41:43.800
[SPEAKER_02]: doesn't work like that because Everyone has a budget. Everyone has a timeline. Procrastination is a thing. And you have to look at your

41:43.920 --> 41:51.768
[SPEAKER_02]: average user and the least educated user. And that's where the 10-year reserve rule comes from. and certain other things should be included

41:51.828 --> 41:59.795
[SPEAKER_02]: and that's where like the the replaceable lines on reserves like making everything as easy as possible for people inexpensive and hassle-free

42:00.336 --> 42:09.882
[SPEAKER_02]: without compromising safety so if like with the replaceable lines if there was some kind of structural downside to having that, then I think

42:10.622 --> 42:19.824
[SPEAKER_02]: that could not be used. But there's no difference structurally. All it adds is additional safety and options for people to replace a line

42:19.924 --> 42:26.905
[SPEAKER_02]: easily, instead of seeing a little bit of abrasion on the line because they had a tree leavening or something, or it got dragged through a bush.

42:26.925 --> 42:35.069
[SPEAKER_02]: And they're like, I don't really want to send it to a factory and have no reserve for a month. I'll just put it back in my harness. So this is where

42:35.169 --> 42:45.333
[SPEAKER_02]: considering all of your users and not just your best users comes in. Do you think this is somewhere where capitalism is making its presence

42:45.393 --> 42:53.017
[SPEAKER_00]: felt, where eventually what I see is paragliding isn't really the cheapest form of aviation anymore. There are much more affordable ways

42:53.077 --> 42:59.943
[SPEAKER_00]: of experiencing the art of flight and it's turning out to be fairly lucrative for the wing manufacturers and the equipment manufacturers

43:00.023 --> 43:07.630
[SPEAKER_00]: and the broader manufacturing industry. Like anybody who's in manufacturing is making fair bit of money with new brands coming out and

43:07.690 --> 43:17.394
[SPEAKER_00]: experimenting. So is this somewhere where it's the more fragile I make? the more Apple kind of a theory that my phones will only be serviced at my

43:17.574 --> 43:27.880
[SPEAKER_00]: service centers. Find obsolescence. Yeah. I don't think it's that specifically, at least for most of them. It's innovation and gear is so

43:27.920 --> 43:38.949
[SPEAKER_02]: good now that... it's easier to get someone to buy your product because you have to assume that most people already have flying gear the best way

43:38.989 --> 43:48.757
[SPEAKER_02]: to get them to to buy yours is to come up with something new that you can tell them and convince them is safer and that's where whether it's a

43:48.777 --> 43:59.331
[SPEAKER_02]: choroid or replaceable line easily replaceable lines on reserve or a slightly lower descent rate or a lighter reserve. That's where that

43:59.351 --> 44:12.486
[SPEAKER_02]: comes in. And sometimes there are valid safety arguments for it. And sometimes it's actually a net negative to the entire safety component.

44:13.524 --> 44:21.033
[SPEAKER_00]: These technologies keep on coming up one after the other and any new technology is having a bit of a marketing angle to it and is not being sold

44:21.073 --> 44:28.862
[SPEAKER_00]: at the cheapest price. So it is being sold under the context that money means safety in your flying and why would you compromise on safety? So

44:28.922 --> 44:37.169
[SPEAKER_00]: that's why I spend this money, get the latest tech on the market and... Have peace of mind. And then you have fiascos like or any other thing.

44:37.850 --> 44:47.015
[SPEAKER_00]: Then basically, I'm looking at this from a consumer's perspective. You go out there, learn this brand new, amazing way of moving. You fall in

44:47.055 --> 44:54.938
[SPEAKER_00]: love with the sport that you've always wanted to. And then you want to secure it while you're trying to secure it. You are given options which

44:55.038 --> 45:02.988
[SPEAKER_00]: are hard to choose from. So as a person who's going to try these, how do you come to that kind of decision making? And where should the thought

45:03.008 --> 45:13.185
[SPEAKER_00]: process of a consumer be in this case? That's a hard answer to give. Initially, you start out buying the gear that your instructor

45:13.225 --> 45:20.451
[SPEAKER_02]: recommends. As you progress in your career, most people end up getting the things that their friends recommend. You'll frequently find

45:20.651 --> 45:34.001
[SPEAKER_02]: groups or clans in each area of people who fly similar gear, but possibly without proper market-wide research. There's a lot of just brand

45:34.322 --> 45:45.815
[SPEAKER_02]: loyalty in the industry. and sometimes blindly trusting their favorite brand. Sometimes that works out, sometimes not so much. One

45:45.895 --> 45:58.026
[SPEAKER_02]: very good metric that I like to tell people about and use personally is looking at a brand's website and seeing if they have a recall page and how

45:58.106 --> 46:08.995
