46:09.055 --> 46:18.141
[SPEAKER_02]: paragliding incidents, and whether or not there is a proper recall done. Then you look at the companies and what they recall things over and

46:18.161 --> 46:27.905
[SPEAKER_02]: basically see how small it is. And if a company is doing a recall for a limited batch of serial numbers, that's a very good thing in my eyes

46:28.806 --> 46:36.649
[SPEAKER_02]: because that shows attention to detail and A lot of times when you send a wing in for certification, there's usually a sheet that you can pull up

46:36.689 --> 46:44.451
[SPEAKER_02]: on the DHB's website, and it shows the individual manufacturers of every single component, every single little piece of tape and webbing

46:44.531 --> 46:53.474
[SPEAKER_02]: and line and pulleys and everything. And the number of manufacturers that it takes to make a wing is massive. There's potential for designer

46:53.514 --> 47:05.359
[SPEAKER_02]: manufacturing issues, but then there's also massive potential for just a bad batch of bullies or lines when a company is very open and

47:05.519 --> 47:15.044
[SPEAKER_02]: transparent about recalls and problems that maybe only occurred once and they'll say one pilot had this issue and they'll show a photo of what

47:15.084 --> 47:22.567
[SPEAKER_02]: happened to that pilot and they'll say if your wing is in the serial number or they will contact all the original owners with that that's a

47:22.607 --> 47:31.068
[SPEAKER_02]: really good thing and a lot of companies don't do that are there companies who do that the best and everyone has a bias and i'm sure there

47:31.088 --> 47:39.830
[SPEAKER_02]: are other companies that do this as well but the two best that i know of are skywalk and ozone if you look on their recalls pages on their websites

47:40.430 --> 47:50.833
[SPEAKER_02]: there are a lot some of them are for very minute tiny things that will not kill you but are some sort of manufacturing or structural failure you

47:50.893 --> 47:59.799
[SPEAKER_02]: can get your gear replaced or repaired by them for free I'm so glad you brought this point up because this circles back to Eric's talk. Both of

47:59.839 --> 48:06.888
[SPEAKER_00]: you would have been such great co-guests in one episode where there would have been such amazing discussions. But we were talking about

48:06.949 --> 48:13.837
[SPEAKER_00]: manufacturers and because he himself is running a brand, Flyoneo, in France, employing everything locally and producing everything

48:13.878 --> 48:21.306
[SPEAKER_00]: there. With all the European laws and norms, we narrowed down to three types of brands where it was like, there are some brands who have their

48:21.346 --> 48:28.374
[SPEAKER_00]: own manufacturing, their own R&D and their own setup. And then there are other ones which kind of outsource, they have their own designing, but

48:28.474 --> 48:36.081
[SPEAKER_00]: outsource the manufacturing to factories in Sri Lanka and Thailand. And then there are other ones which just go to a factory, pick up

48:36.181 --> 48:42.885
[SPEAKER_00]: anything, put their logo on it and start selling. When it comes to decision making, the way you explained the recall strategy of the brand

48:42.925 --> 48:51.712
[SPEAKER_00]: was a very good illustration of admitting that, hey, we messed up and now this is how we're going to make it right. That is one way to choose your

48:51.732 --> 48:59.976
[SPEAKER_00]: brand as a consumer where you find how much trust factor there is. If you are going out there as a student learning paragliding, it came to my mind

48:59.996 --> 49:09.080
[SPEAKER_00]: that it would be such a good idea if schools had their accident list or something that these are the students that came in and messed up. Do you

49:09.100 --> 49:15.202
[SPEAKER_00]: think people should look for something like that as well while choosing to get into the sport? Unfortunately, I don't see that kind of

49:15.242 --> 49:18.804
[SPEAKER_02]: transparency on the instructor school level.

49:22.463 --> 49:37.330
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a bit beyond the scope of what you can hope for. I see it a lot here at Garda. Some, we'll say, bad instructors, and they have incidents, and

49:37.370 --> 49:44.953
[SPEAKER_02]: some really bad ones. The landing area here is 100 meters long, 20 meters wide, grass with water, and you can always go in the water, and it's safe,

49:45.153 --> 49:52.156
[SPEAKER_02]: and the boat will pick you up instantly. There's no risk, essentially. And everyone has a life vest. Still somehow some experienced pilots who

49:52.176 --> 50:02.701
[SPEAKER_02]: came here for an SIV managed to pendulum in and smash into the road that's 10 meters away on the other side away from the lake and break everything.

50:03.542 --> 50:14.125
[SPEAKER_02]: And incidents like that have happened both to good instructors and bad instructors. Sometimes you just have... a client who has a license, but

50:15.405 --> 50:23.692
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't want to say they shouldn't be flying, but it sounds when you say anyone can fly. You can ride a bike, but sometimes that's not true. It's

50:24.352 --> 50:34.920
[SPEAKER_02]: easy to judge from the outside and see who the really good instructors are versus mediocre ones. Sometimes you just get unlucky and get a

50:35.020 --> 50:43.303
[SPEAKER_02]: student who doesn't follow instructions at all. I have personal instructors that I recommend to people and others that I tell them to

50:43.343 --> 50:53.150
[SPEAKER_02]: stay away from. But it's hard to quantify that. Everyone says they're the best and the safest and have the most experience. Do you think we

50:53.170 --> 51:02.735
[SPEAKER_00]: should encourage people asking their instructors, what is your accident record? I think it'd be difficult to actually get an answer.

51:03.175 --> 51:11.161
[SPEAKER_02]: Paragliding incidents in general are very poorly documented. They really do their best to scrape the person off the ground as quickly as

51:11.201 --> 51:22.448
[SPEAKER_02]: possible and keep the thing going afterwards and pretending like nothing happened. Unlike skydiving and base jumping where there is a

51:23.829 --> 51:33.778
[SPEAKER_02]: list of incidents and fatalities, there's a forum for skydiving serious injury ends up getting posted the same day. People are asking

51:33.938 --> 51:42.367
[SPEAKER_02]: what happened and it reflects directly on each drop zone. There's nothing like that for schools. You just hear stories more or less, but

51:42.407 --> 51:51.934
[SPEAKER_02]: the video very rarely actually gets shared. Help me compare this to the base community. Base is pretty much unregulated, right? It's just a

51:51.954 --> 52:00.522
[SPEAKER_00]: bunch of friends. You get in the tribe, you learn the sport. There's no set regularized teaching pattern. It's just personal friendship

52:00.562 --> 52:07.508
[SPEAKER_00]: based. You go meet the right people. And of course, there is a bit of commercialization to it. But the trust factor is placed on person's

52:07.668 --> 52:15.515
[SPEAKER_00]: flying skills and nothing else. And then we have paragliding where everything is by the book. You have proper racing competitions, World

52:15.555 --> 52:23.862
[SPEAKER_00]: Cups and schedules. These big certification companies are like a whole machinery working behind the sport. Tens of thousands of people

52:24.382 --> 52:33.710
[SPEAKER_00]: running the show in various ways as tandem tourism or solo and tandem paragliding. Did all of this structurization mess up The freedom that

52:34.290 --> 52:41.013
[SPEAKER_00]: could have been there with no rules, that is what shows you how beautifully it can blossom if you do not have any restrictions where

52:41.053 --> 52:52.537
[SPEAKER_00]: people set their own restrictions by themselves. yeah unfortunately for us base jumpers most base jumps are illegal it's part sport part like

52:54.438 --> 53:04.567
[SPEAKER_02]: white collar criminal crime trespassing and that's just accepted in paragliding that doesn't work because people want to be able to hike

53:04.627 --> 53:12.911
[SPEAKER_02]: wait for hours for the wind to be perfect and have access to good launches and landings. And I think that's where it all starts from. Because to get

53:12.971 --> 53:20.999
[SPEAKER_02]: insurance, if someone crashes, you need some kind of chain of responsibility. And that starts with the certified gear. With BASE,

53:21.279 --> 53:26.924
[SPEAKER_02]: going back to the instructor thing, even though you don't need an instructor, pretty much everyone gets an instructor now, instead of

53:26.964 --> 53:35.688
[SPEAKER_02]: the old way of having a mentor. There is definitely a wide range of instructors who are known for basically jumping during the day,

53:35.888 --> 53:50.992
[SPEAKER_02]: partying at night. And their ground school curriculum is loose and on the fly and impromptu at every course, as opposed to ones who are more

53:51.092 --> 54:02.536
[SPEAKER_02]: thorough, have an actual syllabus of some kind, some fixed basic set of information with a whiteboard or projector and different scenarios

54:03.036 --> 54:11.565
[SPEAKER_02]: where everyone is taught and shown the basic information they'll need in their career. I prefer instructors who are still current and active

54:12.446 --> 54:22.337
[SPEAKER_02]: and pushing their own knowledge on a daily or weekly basis. It comes down to still flying, still having passion for flying, Still trying all the

54:22.377 --> 54:33.324
[SPEAKER_02]: new gear and wings. And Heli is a good example of this. He flies pretty much every day he can. He's very active personally and as an instructor.

54:33.844 --> 54:42.630
[SPEAKER_02]: And there are a lot of other ones that I see where I've literally never seen them fly. I've seen them dozens of times instructing. I've never

54:42.690 --> 54:50.470
[SPEAKER_02]: seen them in the air. I'm sure they're good at... talking to people on the radio and telling them what they're doing wrong. But their level of

54:50.850 --> 55:05.452
[SPEAKER_02]: unbiased, neutral knowledge of gear and safety standards has remained fixed for 10, 15, 20 years. That's my main criteria for instructors. I

55:05.472 --> 55:13.754
[SPEAKER_02]: choose an instructor who knows all the latest gear, will give you unbiased gear recommendations and not just the gear they sell that

55:13.814 --> 55:21.281
[SPEAKER_02]: gives them the highest margins. This is so hard to make a decision on as an individual pilot when you go out looking for an instructor. It's hard to

55:21.301 --> 55:31.472
[SPEAKER_00]: find the courage to question that, hey, how much do you love what you're teaching? Because I signed up for an SIV course with an instructor who

55:31.792 --> 55:41.356
[SPEAKER_00]: just knew how to say the names of the maneuvers bro like he put us in a room put on a jockey sanderson video on the tv and then started explaining the

55:41.396 --> 55:49.680
[SPEAKER_00]: video and then said that go out and you know you watch what he does in the video now do the same and i will say the name of the maneuver and then you do

55:49.760 --> 56:00.068
[SPEAKER_00]: that's it that guy definitely could not do the menu like siv himself but he was still teaching it And I was stupid enough to not just do my research

56:00.128 --> 56:06.977
[SPEAKER_00]: and say that when was the last time you stalled your wing? But everything went well, though. But it's important to have the courage. You can have

56:06.997 --> 56:15.980
[SPEAKER_02]: the courage to ask, but then... whether you'll get an honest, and that's the thing, an honest answer. I can think of a few humble, very good

56:16.020 --> 56:25.884
[SPEAKER_02]: instructors who if you ask them this question, they will probably be honest with you and their answer will maybe scare you. When you compare

56:25.924 --> 56:34.908
[SPEAKER_02]: it to some other instructors who are not honest with you, it'll probably sound worse. And that's where the personal bias and humility of, yes,

56:35.169 --> 56:43.474
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm an instructor, yes, I'm an expert pilot, knowing that, but you would never say that. If you pitch it to five instructors and you're like,

56:43.874 --> 56:54.843
[SPEAKER_02]: convince me that you are the best instructor out here, the results, what you're told, and the truth may be inversed. Unfortunately, that's

56:54.883 --> 57:05.476
[SPEAKER_02]: where so much of flying and personal safety comes down to picking the right friends to get advice from and do what they tell you until you know

57:05.916 --> 57:10.742
[SPEAKER_02]: better yourself. Do you think blockchain will solve this?

57:13.923 --> 57:24.947
[SPEAKER_02]: Absolutely. When everyone, when every instructor mints their own NFT and you can actively bid on expensive NFT to get a full training course.

57:24.967 --> 57:31.709
[SPEAKER_00]: You have a flight log on the blockchain where you can see everybody's flight, how much airtime they have, what places they have flown and how

57:31.749 --> 57:37.631
[SPEAKER_00]: much they're flying. Maybe that's a good use case scenario of AI and new technologies. Maybe we should make it on PowerLighting Atlas to begin

57:37.651 --> 57:37.811
[SPEAKER_00]: with.

57:40.829 --> 57:49.491
[SPEAKER_02]: And yeah, it's one of those things that people now I'm trying to post more because like I am a rigger, an airplane pilot, software engineer, I've

57:49.551 --> 58:00.274
[SPEAKER_02]: done so many things I actually have people think I don't have a pilot license, or flying license for anything that I do. Just randomly pull

58:00.354 --> 58:10.521
[SPEAKER_02]: out gear and try it on the same day. I've tried to expose more behind-the-scenes stuff. What you see online versus what I actually am

58:10.862 --> 58:27.556
[SPEAKER_02]: and say and do in face-to-face is two very different things. It's refreshing seeing some other people in person giving such terrible

58:27.716 --> 58:39.140
[SPEAKER_02]: safety advice compared to what I now try to promote and push the stunts or things that I do where people don't know all the details. A lot of times,

58:39.260 --> 58:47.122
[SPEAKER_02]: by the time people have as much flying experience as I have, they're sponsored. The problem with being sponsored is then you are completely

58:47.182 --> 58:50.302
[SPEAKER_02]: biased and contractually obligated to remain so biased.

58:54.381 --> 59:03.948
[SPEAKER_02]: for so many things. If your brand, if your sponsored brand doesn't make it, if you're asked a question and it's something that exists in another

59:03.988 --> 59:12.471
[SPEAKER_02]: company but not in yours, the answer is no, ours is better. And I happen to live in a place where I see tons of accidents and incidents and very

59:12.571 --> 59:27.379
[SPEAKER_02]: unique failure modes of gear. I can discuss that. I have no problem. I've seen enough carnage and gore that I'm quite desensitized and honest

59:27.419 --> 59:36.544
[SPEAKER_02]: with it instead of people who try to pretend that certain problems or failure modes don't exist. Another good topic is the difference

59:36.584 --> 59:45.928
[SPEAKER_02]: between perceived versus actual risk. For a lot of pilots, what they're actually worried about and what they should be worried about are two

59:46.249 --> 59:54.653
[SPEAKER_02]: opposite things. So interesting. I just don't feel like moving forward because this is such an enriching talk. We should write an encyclopedia

59:54.693 --> 01:00:03.102
[SPEAKER_00]: together one day on this. Yeah, someday. I keep threatening to write a book. Someday I will write a book. Yeah, that's another collaboration

01:00:03.122 --> 01:00:12.494
[SPEAKER_00]: we can make in time to come. But for now, enlighten us, Gabe. How do you handle criticism when things do not go as per plan? And how do you learn

01:00:12.574 --> 01:00:21.549
[SPEAKER_00]: from your mistakes? And how do you get a better version and become who you are today? So I guess initially I was like everyone else and didn't

01:00:21.589 --> 01:00:32.300
[SPEAKER_02]: really post crash videos and mistakes. I slowly took responsibility. There was a point where people would tell me that I shouldn't post it

01:00:32.420 --> 01:00:41.383
[SPEAKER_02]: because other people were going to copy it. And one of the reasons that I switched to being very transparent and posting all of my fails, all my

01:00:41.403 --> 01:00:50.365
[SPEAKER_02]: crashes, all my near-death incidents, is to show the good and the bad. Show people what it looks like when everything goes according to plan,

01:00:50.505 --> 01:00:59.187
[SPEAKER_02]: and also what it looks like when you didn't think of one little thing, or you did something that you had done plenty of times before, but that time

01:00:59.227 --> 01:01:10.076
[SPEAKER_02]: it went wrong. like flying my nine meter speed wing into clouds for several minutes and suddenly I'm facing a mountain and crashing

01:01:10.396 --> 01:01:20.697
[SPEAKER_02]: straight into it. A lot of people have posted videos of scary cloud incidents. This is the last time I'm ever going to do this. They fly out

01:01:20.717 --> 01:01:29.380
[SPEAKER_02]: from clouds. And I had done that a whole bunch of times before with a GPS watch. I got very comfortable with it. And then one day my watch failed

01:01:29.920 --> 01:01:39.824
[SPEAKER_02]: and the results were terrifying. I will never fly in clouds again. And I posted that video the same day and took full responsibility for it. I

01:01:39.884 --> 01:01:52.129
[SPEAKER_02]: explained my reasoning. I also explained what led me to become so comfortable flying into clouds, which in that case was that season, the

01:01:52.409 --> 01:02:00.033
[SPEAKER_02]: cable car lift ticket system changed to where you would have to pay double the price to go back down if there were clouds. For tandems, that

01:02:00.073 --> 01:02:07.600
[SPEAKER_02]: meant if a tandem had to cancel, it would cost them 50 or 60 euros total, and they wouldn't make any money from the tandem. That season, there

01:02:07.620 --> 01:02:16.728
[SPEAKER_02]: were more acropilots and tandems flying right into clouds where they disappear five or 10 seconds after takeoff and then pop out somewhere

01:02:16.869 --> 01:02:25.730
[SPEAKER_02]: further out of the cloud. than there ever had been in any previous season here. And I started doing that and just became very familiar with that.

01:02:26.511 --> 01:02:36.094
[SPEAKER_02]: And that same season, there were at least two incidents of tandems who did that and also crashed in the mountain. And the world will never see

01:02:36.114 --> 01:02:48.558
[SPEAKER_02]: those videos. And even locally, personally, it was very few people were told about this. And it was very much a don't tell anyone. thing ends and

01:02:48.578 --> 01:02:55.381
[SPEAKER_00]: luckily everyone survived one of the tandem pairs ended up in the hospital are you telling me this is happening in in one of the most

01:02:55.982 --> 01:03:04.786
[SPEAKER_00]: governed spots of our sport it's hard to believe what i was like the reason why i asked this is that is this like herd mentality eventually

01:03:04.826 --> 01:03:13.931
[SPEAKER_00]: taking the best of us, regardless of how skilled we are, how much work we put in, and how carefully we tread. If we are surrounded by people who are

01:03:14.011 --> 01:03:23.677
[SPEAKER_00]: collectively normalizing something, we tend to become part of the herd and follow as well. Yeah, absolutely. Just like the thing of you

01:03:23.717 --> 01:03:32.359
[SPEAKER_02]: becoming the average of your five closest friends, you'll also become the average of the people that you fly with. And if a lot of people around

01:03:32.399 --> 01:03:44.304
[SPEAKER_02]: you are flying in a dangerous way, then at some point you will also become desensitized to what you were taught originally and what you know is the

01:03:44.524 --> 01:03:53.909
[SPEAKER_02]: safe way to fly. Help me apply this to the Acro team that was inventing the sport where they were looked upon as rebels and like somebody doing

01:03:53.949 --> 01:03:59.392
[SPEAKER_00]: really stupid. So if you were a part of that bunch, you would be like, you're going to end up killing yourself. However, eventually pushing

01:03:59.433 --> 01:04:07.739
[SPEAKER_00]: their own boundaries. I wouldn't, I wouldn't say knowingly, but of course, like cloud, navigating the cloud was knowingly putting

01:04:07.779 --> 01:04:15.163
[SPEAKER_00]: yourself in risk. But even then Acro was like knowingly putting yourself in configurations where things could go wrong. So it is the

01:04:15.203 --> 01:04:27.228
[SPEAKER_00]: same mentality is what my curiosity is that is this needed to progress in the sport or a pinch of this or like this, but up to a limited extent, this

01:04:27.288 --> 01:04:38.698
[SPEAKER_00]: kind of mindset, if you want to progress in the sport. Well, so two things that occurred to me specifically with Acro. One, when Teo first did

01:04:38.979 --> 01:04:47.886
[SPEAKER_02]: Super Stall to Infinite, which was incredible and a true original innovation that a lot of people had thought about and talked about, but

01:04:47.946 --> 01:04:56.813
[SPEAKER_02]: no one had ever actually done. That was a huge step. Since then, I've seen a whole bunch of people trying it and destroying gliders and getting

01:04:56.893 --> 01:05:07.084
[SPEAKER_02]: wrapped in gliders. just to be able to say that they can do it. Like people who do not compete and will never compete and have a fraction of the skill

01:05:07.144 --> 01:05:17.529
[SPEAKER_02]: that Teo does but they just happen to own or a glider that they think is capable of it and they just want to do the cool new thing. That I

01:05:17.889 --> 01:05:26.654
[SPEAKER_02]: personally disagree with on a personal level and think it's not worth the risk to try to prove that you can do something that Teo already did and

01:05:26.934 --> 01:05:34.501
[SPEAKER_02]: innovated and created. Since then, a whole bunch of other people have did. But that like you personally, there's no, I don't know, I guess some

01:05:34.541 --> 01:05:42.247
[SPEAKER_02]: people get satisfaction. But for me, just following the herd and being able to say, yes, I can do this super stall to infinite. It's not worth

01:05:42.527 --> 01:05:49.532
[SPEAKER_02]: falling into your glider and destroying gliders and potentially destroying yourself. and potentially causing yourself a whole lot of

01:05:49.572 --> 01:05:58.436
[SPEAKER_02]: PTSD and trauma, speaking personally, from ending up in freefall wrapped in your glider, trying to pig yourself out and throw out a

01:05:58.496 --> 01:06:06.280
[SPEAKER_02]: reserve or a base canopy or something. That's definitely not worth it. The other thing is specifically about acro-based cutaway harnesses.

01:06:07.299 --> 01:06:18.708
[SPEAKER_02]: And this is something that I have a discussion about fairly regularly with people who are semi-experienced. But there's this curve where you

01:06:18.728 --> 01:06:25.654
[SPEAKER_02]: have enough experience where you've gone through a normal acro harness with two reserves. And you have a few friends around you who have an

01:06:25.734 --> 01:06:35.839
[SPEAKER_02]: acro-based cutaway. And you've talked to Raul and some other people who use them or Vesso. and you think that's the final step in your acro career

01:06:35.879 --> 01:06:42.981
[SPEAKER_02]: and progression, specifically for safety. And I've seen some people get it with two years of flying experience. They're good pilots and fly

01:06:43.021 --> 01:06:50.922
[SPEAKER_02]: every day, but they do not know what they do not know. They have no skydiving or base jumping experience. I used to make a decent amount of

01:06:50.962 --> 01:07:02.467
[SPEAKER_02]: money packing acrobates harnesses for people like over 200 euros the standard here in garda at the time was 100 euros for a the just the acro

01:07:02.707 --> 01:07:14.215
[SPEAKER_02]: base canopy pack job and then you throw in a regalo and around and that's over 200 euros from people who have it and use it but have no idea how to

01:07:14.235 --> 01:07:20.383
[SPEAKER_02]: pack it and probably have never read the manual. One of the conditions that I used to have with people for packing it for them is that they had to

01:07:20.423 --> 01:07:28.088
[SPEAKER_02]: be there and I was not packing it for them. I was teaching them how to pack it. yeah and that for me was my way of relieving myself of the

01:07:28.128 --> 01:07:34.973
[SPEAKER_02]: responsibility of them potentially using it wrong and killing themselves one time when i saw a girl that i had packed for almost kill

01:07:34.994 --> 01:07:41.559
[SPEAKER_02]: herself because she left the speed bar hooked up and she didn't have a speed bar cut away and she cut away from the ground looking at it she just

01:07:41.599 --> 01:07:50.526
[SPEAKER_02]: goes into free fall with a canopy flagged out in a streamer and no cutaway happening and fortunately a few seconds later she threw one of her

01:07:50.566 --> 01:07:59.131
[SPEAKER_02]: rounds but as who was the last person to touch that pack job that was very scary and made me decide to stop packing for people the number of people

01:07:59.171 --> 01:08:14.404
[SPEAKER_02]: who have died on acrobase setups i i personally believe exceeds the number of people that the acrobase harness has saved what the Wow. There

01:08:14.424 --> 01:08:22.926
[SPEAKER_02]: are some very specific scenarios in places like El Hierro where being under a base canopy is statistically safer than being under a round.

01:08:23.186 --> 01:08:32.248
[SPEAKER_02]: Some are super windy with rocks and a really gnarly place to potentially crash land with high vertical and horizontal descent rates. What

01:08:32.288 --> 01:08:43.609
[SPEAKER_02]: people don't realize is you are flying every time with basically a stunt rig. You need to train for it. You need to maintain it. You need to inspect

01:08:43.669 --> 01:08:52.873
[SPEAKER_02]: it after and before every single flight. And there are a lot of people who don't. And the number of total cutaway failures where they pull the

01:08:52.893 --> 01:08:59.896
[SPEAKER_02]: cutaway cable out and they're holding it completely, but the rings just don't release because they're locked in either because they use the

01:08:59.916 --> 01:09:11.441
[SPEAKER_02]: wrong carabiner. It's a three ring system, not a carabiner and two ring system. Or because they haven't made the, sorry, I happen to have it

01:09:11.481 --> 01:09:19.128
[SPEAKER_02]: right here, but this is the ring that you're actually like, this is a real three ring. And this comes from skydiving. Unfortunately, this is

01:09:19.188 --> 01:09:27.035
[SPEAKER_02]: custom made and cannot be used for a paraglider. You have to use this other one that I have. So instead people go to a sailing or climbing shop

01:09:27.496 --> 01:09:36.684
[SPEAKER_02]: and buy a round shackle. that looks similar and is roughly the right dimensions. They check it on the ground and it works. And at some point

01:09:36.784 --> 01:09:48.172
[SPEAKER_02]: they need it and maybe it works. There are also dimensional inaccuracies and manufacturing and design defects in a lot of the V3

01:09:48.212 --> 01:09:58.883
[SPEAKER_02]: rings on Acrobase systems where they use either webbing that's too stiff or the middle ring webbing is too long. So it gets this memory shape

01:09:59.063 --> 01:10:08.589
[SPEAKER_02]: where the big ring just sits in it and is perfectly happy staying there under tension even when you pull the cutaway. Some other people have

01:10:08.669 --> 01:10:20.856
[SPEAKER_02]: died, including someone that I knew who was a expert space jumper, speed flyer, acro pilot. And he was just going for a casual recreational

01:10:20.936 --> 01:10:31.102
[SPEAKER_02]: cutaway, like a planned cutaway. And he went and he pulled. And unfortunately, he rigged the static line bridle incorrectly. And he

01:10:31.142 --> 01:10:39.924
[SPEAKER_02]: just went into free fall and impacted a few seconds later. And that was someone who was experienced, who was a skydiver and base jumper, was

01:10:40.024 --> 01:10:48.811
[SPEAKER_02]: planning to use the cutaway, and he still screwed up and died. That's more experience and more planning than a lot of pilots that I've seen.

01:10:49.612 --> 01:10:57.477
[SPEAKER_02]: There are a lot of people who had zero training, and even if their gear is rigged by someone who packed it, like me, who knows what they're doing,

01:10:58.098 --> 01:11:03.302
[SPEAKER_02]: and even though I've done it a bunch of times, I still have the manual open, and I still verify everything, because every harness is a little

01:11:03.322 --> 01:11:13.549
[SPEAKER_02]: bit different. then I grill people, or I used to after I'd pack for them, I'd be like, okay, you have an emergency, you fall into the glider, it's a

01:11:13.649 --> 01:11:23.359
[SPEAKER_02]: few seconds, and you're falling, and you see a couple lines around you. Do you cut away or do you throw around? The correct answer is you throw

01:11:23.379 --> 01:11:33.568
[SPEAKER_02]: around. If you see any bit of fabric or nylon or dyneema around you still attached to you, going around you to the harness, you cannot cut away

01:11:33.608 --> 01:11:41.354
[SPEAKER_02]: then. Then you're turning. Basically, it's the concepts. It comes from skydiving, where you're turning a sort of low-speed malfunction into a

01:11:41.434 --> 01:11:48.060
[SPEAKER_02]: high-speed malfunction. You have some fabric over your head. You're still connected to it. and then you cut away, but you're still partially

